    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe RFC7807 part of the change is structurally clean and idiomatic: introducing `OwnerCreationRejectedException` as a status-carrying exception and letting `ExceptionControllerAdvice` render it is the natural Spring MVC pattern, the new handler reuses the existing `detailBuild` helper (so the body shape stays consistent with the other handlers), and the controller's `return new ResponseEntity<>(status)` \u2192 `throw ...` swap keeps the rejection logic in the right unit. No real complaint there.\n\nThe one structural problem is the `Postcodes.java` edit:\n\n- **Unrelated change bundled into the changeset.** `Postcodes.isValidForCity` adding a `city == null` null-guard has nothing to do with the spec (RFC7807 bodies for 400/409/429 rejections). It's a defensive correctness fix in a validation utility, on a code path that would otherwise surface as a 500 (`handleGeneralException`), not one of the rejection statuses this change is about. Mixing it in hurts the coherence of the change \u2014 a maintainer reading this diff would reasonably ask why a postcode null-guard is in a \"problem+json for rejections\" change. Split it into its own commit/change so this one stays single-purpose.\n\nMinor, optional (not blocking):\n- The exception is named for owner *creation* but is a generic `(HttpStatus, detail)` carrier. That's fine while it has exactly one call site family; just be aware that if a second feature reaches for it, the name will fight its generality. No action needed now.",
